Q: How do I fix this horrible UV bug in my JMonkeyEngine game? A: <p><code>Atlas.getAtlas()</code> plus <code>atl.setWrap(Texture.WrapMode.Repeat)</code> cannot repeat one tile of the atlas. The repeat state applies to the whole atlas texture, so if <code>manageUV</code> wraps the final atlas UV, the last quad on a chunk's positive <code>x</code>/<code>z</code> side can interpolate from near the tile's <code>maxU/maxV</code> back to <code>minU/minV</code>. That wrapped jump is the edge/corner discontinuity you are seeing.</p>
<p>Keep two different UVs: a local repeat UV, and the atlas rectangle. Apply the repeat before converting into atlas space, preferably in the fragment shader:</p>
<pre><code>glsl
uniform sampler2D m_DiffuseMap;

varying vec2 vLocalUv;    // unwrapped local/world UV, e.g. based on ax/az
varying vec2 vAtlasMin;   // tile lower-left in Atlas.getAtlas()
varying vec2 vAtlasSize;  // vec2(maxU - minU, maxV - minV)

vec4 sampleAtlasTile() {
    vec2 inTile = fract(vLocalUv);              // repeat inside this tile
    vec2 atlasUv = vAtlasMin + inTile * vAtlasSize;
    return texture2D(m_DiffuseMap, atlasUv);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Do not put the already-wrapped atlas-space value into <code>VertexBuffer.Type.TexCoord</code> for a quad that crosses a repeat boundary. Vertex interpolation will sample across the atlas rectangle.</p>
<p>If the repeat must be baked into the mesh, split each face at the tile-repeat seams so no triangle crosses from <code>maxU</code> back to <code>minU</code>. The other practical option is not to atlas textures that need hardware repetition: use a texture array or separate repeatable textures for those blocks.</p>
