Q: How to fix the position with html and css flexbox and position? A: <p>The issue is that your two .horizontal divs and the &lt;p&gt; are direct children of .contenu-central, so they stack according to that container’s layout instead of sitting on one row. Don’t try to fix this with position: absolute — wrap the three elements in their own flex row and let flexbox do the alignment:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;section&gt;
  &lt;div class=&quot;contenu-central&quot;&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;divider&quot;&gt;
      &lt;div class=&quot;horizontal&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;ΔΑΙΜΩΝ · TARTARUS&lt;/p&gt;
      &lt;div class=&quot;horizontal&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div class=&quot;title-block&quot;&gt;
      &lt;h2&gt;red &lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;demon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Import a JSON file to JS code, compatible with old and current browsers A: <p>By now all browsers, especially Chromium-based browsers are evergreen, which means they're automatically updated. You shouldn't have a problem <em>unless</em> something prevents automatic upgrading.</p>
<p>You may be able to use a poly-fill, like the <a href="https://github.com/guybedford/es-module-shims#json-modules" rel="nofollow noreferrer">JSON import feature</a> of <a href="https://github.com/guybedford/es-module-shims" rel="nofollow noreferrer">es-module-shims</a> :</p>
<pre><code>&lt;script async src=&quot;https://ga.jspm.io/npm:es-module-shims@2.8.2/dist/es-module-shims.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;script type=&quot;module&quot;&gt;
import json from 'https://example.com/data.json' with { type: 'json' };
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Whether that works though depends on the actual browser versions and <em>why</em> they can't be upgraded. If Internet access isn't available, you'll have to bundle the polyfill. If the browsers are too old for es-module-shims, you may have to search for another alternative.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://github.com/guybedford/es-module-shims#browser-support" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Browser support</a> section shows that JSON imports are available for Chrome 63+, Firefox 67+ and Safari 11.1+.</p>

